// The heap: each thread has one of its own, a record that its context names (contextHeapOffset) and that its first alloc
// makes, so an alloc and a free take no lock and touch no word another thread writes. The process heap's lock would
// serialize the workers, and a call into it costs more than the whole of a class's alloc.
//
// A box of up to largestSmallBytes is a slot of a size class, and a larger one is a run of the page heap of its own
// (pages.maxon), which is where every block of a heap comes from on every system. A freed large box's run is kept by the
// freeing thread's heap for a large box to come, as Go's per-processor page cache and mimalloc's and snmalloc's caches keep
// theirs, so that a program that grows and drops buffers takes no lock for each: a heap keeps a few runs and at most
// largeKeptBytes, the shortest that fits serves a box, and the rest go back to the page heap, as they all do when the
// thread stops for want of work (releaseKept). A large box that grows takes the pages after its run when they are free
// (growInPlace), so a buffer that grows to megabytes is neither copied nor faulted in again at each step.
// Each class keeps the addresses of the slots freed in it on a stack of its own, so a free and the next alloc of the class
// are a push and a pop; a class whose stack is empty takes a new slot from the end of the heap's current chunk, a run of
// the page heap that every class of the heap carves from, so boxes allocated together lie together. The
// stack is apart from the slots, not a list linked through them, so an alloc reads nothing of a slot that may have left
// the cache since it was freed: it only writes it.
//
// Every box is preceded by a header word: the address of its heap's record plus its class's key (heapLargeKey for a large
// box). The record is aligned to heapAlignBytes, above every key, so free finds the class and the heap from the box
// alone; a mask over the box's address would save the word, but only with chunks aligned to their size, which the page
// heap's runs are not. A slot keeps its class and heap for good, so a reused slot's header is never written again.
//
// A green thread can free what another thread allocated, since it runs on whichever thread holds a processor when it runs.
// A box that the freeing thread's heap does not own goes on its own heap's list of remote frees (heapRemoteOffset), linked
// through the dead box's first word, as mimalloc's thread-free list is; the owner takes the whole list with one exchange
// when a class of its heap runs out, and frees each box there into its class. Only the owner takes from the list and it
// takes everything, so a push cannot see a box that was taken and pushed again under it. The freeing thread gathers the
// boxes of one owner in a batch of its own heap's and pushes remoteBatchBoxes of them, or the batch it has when it frees
// a box of another owner, with one compare-exchange, as snmalloc batches its remote frees: the messages of a service are
// allocated by its senders and freed by whichever threads run them, and a push per box would have all of those threads
// fight for the owner's one word. A batch holds at most remoteBatchBoxes boxes back from their owner, per thread.
// A large box's run, whichever thread made it, goes back from the thread that frees it, to that thread's kept runs, which
// only it reads and writes.
//
// The runtime is the same code whatever the build: the heap counts the slots it carves and the large boxes it holds, less
// those it freed, and the live boxes are that sum over the threads' heaps less the slots on their stacks and their lists
// (liveBoxes), so a box of a class costs no count; only an executable built with --count-allocations asks for it, at the
// end (checkLeaks).

// noHeap is a context's heap before the thread's first alloc has made it.
let noHeap = 0 as Count

// boxHeaderBytes is the header before every box.
let boxHeaderBytes = wordBytes

// heapKeyMask keeps the key of a box's header, and heapRecordMask the address of its heap's record: the bits below and
// above heapAlignBytes.
let heapKeyMask = 0x1FF as Count

let heapRecordMask = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFE00 as Count

// The classes: up to linearLimitBytes, one per word of size (8, 16, ..., 256), since most boxes are records of a few
// words; above it, classesPerDoubling per power of two (320, 384, 448, 512, 640, ...), which keeps a slot at most a
// quarter larger than its box, up to largestSmallBytes. A buffer that an array doubles is a power of two, which the
// ladder holds exactly. The class of the n-th size has the key n words, so a linear class's key is its size.
let classStepBytes = 8 as Count

let classStepShift = 3 as Count

// smallestBoxBytes is the least room a slot has for its box, so that alloc zeroes the first two words of a box it
// reuses with no test of its size: most boxes are of two or three words.
let smallestBoxBytes = 16 as Count

// wordRoundMask clears the bits below a word, rounding a size that has had classStepBytes - 1 added up to a word.
let wordRoundMask = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8 as Count

let linearLimitBytes = 256 as Count

let classesPerDoubling = 4 as Count

// firstDoublingStepShift is the shift of the step between the classes of the first power of two above linearLimitBytes,
// a quarter of the power: 64 of 512.
let firstDoublingStepShift = 6 as Count

let largestSmallBytes = 8192 as Count

// lastKey is the key of the class of largestSmallBytes: 32 linear classes and classesPerDoubling for each power of two
// from 512 to largestSmallBytes, 52 classes of a word each.
let lastKey = 416 as Count

// chunkBytes is the run of the page heap that a heap's classes carve their slots from once the heap has grown, whose pages
// cost nothing until a slot is used. A heap's first chunk is firstChunkBytes, two pages, which hold a slot of the largest
// class, and each next one twice the last up to chunkBytes, since the page heap commits the whole of a run it gives out: a
// program that allocates a few boxes holds a few pages, not a megabyte.
let chunkBytes = 1048576 as Count

let firstChunkBytes = 16384 as Count

// largeBoxPadBytes is what a large box's run holds before the box: the header in its last word, and a word before it so
// that the box keeps the 16-byte alignment a buffer that a loop walks wants. That word holds the length of the run
// (largeBoxLengthOffset), its pages' bytes, which giving it back takes.
let largeBoxPadBytes = 16 as Count

let largeBoxLengthOffset = 0 as Count

// firstStackBytes is the size of a class's first stack of its own, 512 addresses; a full stack doubles.
let firstStackBytes = 4096 as Count

// largeKeptBytes bounds the large runs a heap keeps for reuse, so that a thread that freed much gives the memory back to
// the page heap, where every thread can reuse it: 512 KB, the 64 pages of Go's per-processor page cache. A larger bound
// only adds to a program's peak (4 MB a thread adds 12 MB to a build of self/) for no gain in its time.
let largeKeptBytes = 524288 as Count

// largeKeptWasteShift says how much longer than a large box's run a kept run that serves it may be: a quarter, as a slot
// of a class is at most a quarter larger than its box.
let largeKeptWasteShift = 2 as Count

// remoteBatchBoxes is how many boxes of another heap a heap's batch gathers before it puts them on that heap's list.
let remoteBatchBoxes = 64 as Count

// noSlot is no slot of a heap's kept large blocks: every slot's offset is past the start of the record.
let noSlot = 0 as Count

// alloc is a new zeroed box of a number of bytes.
function alloc(bytes Count) returns Count
	let heap = __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.threadContext(), offset: contextHeapOffset)
	if heap == noHeap 'first'
		return allocFirst(bytes)
	end 'first'
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapAllocsOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapAllocsOffset), right: 1))
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapAllocBytesOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapAllocBytesOffset), right: bytes))
	var key = __Raw.wrappingAdd(bytes, right: classStepBytes - 1) and wordRoundMask
	if bytes > linearLimitBytes 'above'
		if bytes > largestSmallBytes 'large'
			return allocLarge(heap, bytes: bytes, zeroFrom: 0)
		end 'large'
		key = doublingKey(bytes)
	end 'above'
	let top = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: key)
	let box = __Raw.loadWord(top, offset: 0)
	if box == noBox 'fresh'
		return allocFresh(heap, key: key, bytes: bytes)
	end 'fresh'
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: key, value: __Raw.wrappingSub(top, right: wordBytes))
	__Raw.storeWord(box, offset: 0, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(box, offset: wordBytes, value: 0)
	if bytes > smallestBoxBytes 'more'
		var done = smallestBoxBytes
		while done < bytes 'zero'
			__Raw.storeWord(box, offset: done, value: 0)
			done = __Raw.wrappingAdd(done, right: wordBytes)
		end 'zero'
	end 'more'
	return box
end 'alloc'

// allocFirst is the first alloc of a thread, which makes its heap.
function allocFirst(bytes Count) returns Count
	_ = newHeapRecord()
	return alloc(bytes)
end 'allocFirst'

// grownBox is a buffer of bytes that holds the first kept bytes of box, a buffer of held bytes or noBox: box itself where
// its run grows in place (growInPlace), and else a new box with them copied in, the old one the caller's to free, since
// what is written next may be read from it. With zeroed, its bytes past kept read zero, as a new box's do, box's from kept
// to held having been zero already; without, for a caller that reads nothing past what it writes, nothing is zeroed. A
// large box zeroes no more than that, as Go's growslice clears only what its copy does not write; a slot of a class is
// zeroed whole, which costs little. A box of more than largestSmallBytes is a large one, so held alone says which. Where
// nothing is kept, as when an empty array first grows, it is a new box, with nothing to grow in place or copy.
function grownBox(box Count, held Count, kept Count, bytes Count, zeroed bool) returns Count
	if kept == 0 'nothing'
		return alloc(bytes)
	end 'nothing'
	var moved = noBox
	if bytes <= largestSmallBytes 'small'
		moved = alloc(bytes)
	end 'small' else 'large'
		if held > largestSmallBytes 'inPlace'
			var heldZeroFrom = bytes
			if zeroed 'zeroed'
				heldZeroFrom = held
			end 'zeroed'
			if growInPlace(box, zeroFrom: heldZeroFrom, bytes: bytes) 'grew'
				return box
			end 'grew'
		end 'inPlace'
		var zeroFrom = bytes
		if zeroed 'zeroed'
			zeroFrom = kept
		end 'zeroed'
		let heap = heapMade(__Raw.loadWord(__Raw.threadContext(), offset: contextHeapOffset))
		countAlloc(heap, bytes: bytes)
		moved = allocLarge(heap, bytes: bytes, zeroFrom: zeroFrom)
	end 'large'
	copyBytes(box, target: moved, count: kept)
	return moved
end 'grownBox'

// growInPlace makes a large box hold a number of bytes where it is, and says whether it did: when its run holds them, or
// the page heap extends its run with the free pages after it. The box's bytes from zeroFrom on are then zero: zeroFrom is
// where the bytes the caller keeps or writes end, and a caller that reads nothing past them passes bytes. The box stays
// where it was, so what points into it stays good.
function growInPlace(box Count, zeroFrom Count, bytes Count) returns bool
	let block = __Raw.wrappingSub(box, right: largeBoxPadBytes)
	let length = __Raw.loadWord(block, offset: largeBoxLengthOffset)
	let held = __Raw.wrappingSub(length, right: largeBoxPadBytes)
	if bytes > held 'extend'
		let wanted = largeBlockBytes(bytes)
		if not pageExtend(runningPages(), run: block, count: length shr pageShift, more: __Raw.wrappingSub(wanted, right: length) shr pageShift, zeroFrom: __Raw.wrappingAdd(largeBoxPadBytes, right: zeroFrom), zeroTo: __Raw.wrappingAdd(largeBoxPadBytes, right: bytes)) 'taken'
			return false
		end 'taken'
		__Raw.storeWord(block, offset: largeBoxLengthOffset, value: wanted)
		zeroRange(box, from: zeroFrom, to: held)
		return true
	end 'extend'
	zeroRange(box, from: zeroFrom, to: bytes)
	return true
end 'growInPlace'

// heapPages is the page heap that a heap's record names, the process's.
function heapPages(heap Count) returns Count
	return __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapPagesOffset)
end 'heapPages'

// free gives a box back to its class, or a large one's block back (freeLarge). An empty place is skipped. A box of another
// thread's heap goes on that heap's list.
function free(box Count)
	if box == noBox 'empty'
		return
	end 'empty'
	let heap = __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.threadContext(), offset: contextHeapOffset)
	let header = __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.wrappingSub(box, right: boxHeaderBytes), offset: 0)
	if (header and heapRecordMask) != heap 'other'
		freeOther(heap, box: box, header: header)
		return
	end 'other'
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapFreesOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapFreesOffset), right: 1))
	let key = header and heapKeyMask
	let top = __Raw.wrappingAdd(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: key), right: wordBytes)
	if top == __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapLimitsOffset)) 'full'
		freeFull(heap, key: key, box: box)
		return
	end 'full'
	__Raw.storeWord(top, offset: 0, value: box)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: key, value: top)
end 'free'

// freeFull is a free that the stack of the box's key has no room for: a large box, whose key's stack is always full, or
// a box of a class whose stack must grow first.
function freeFull(heap Count, key Count, box Count)
	if key == heapLargeKey 'large'
		freeLarge(heap, box: box)
		return
	end 'large'
	let top = growStack(heap, key: key)
	__Raw.storeWord(top, offset: 0, value: box)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: key, value: top)
end 'freeFull'

// freeOther frees a box that the running thread's heap (heap, or noHeap before its first alloc) did not make: a large box's
// block goes back from the running thread, and a slot onto the list of the heap that owns it.
function freeOther(heap Count, box Count, header Count)
	let mine = heapMade(heap)
	addHeapWord(mine, offset: heapFreesOffset, change: 1)
	if (header and heapKeyMask) == heapLargeKey 'large'
		freeLarge(mine, box: box)
		return
	end 'large'
	let owner = header and heapRecordMask
	if __Raw.loadWord(mine, offset: heapBatchOwnerOffset) != owner 'owner'
		flushBatch(mine)
		__Raw.storeWord(mine, offset: heapBatchOwnerOffset, value: owner)
	end 'owner'
	let count = __Raw.loadWord(mine, offset: heapBatchCountOffset)
	if count == 0 'first'
		__Raw.storeWord(mine, offset: heapBatchLastOffset, value: box)
	end 'first'
	__Raw.storeWord(box, offset: 0, value: __Raw.loadWord(mine, offset: heapBatchOffset))
	__Raw.storeWord(mine, offset: heapBatchOffset, value: box)
	__Raw.storeWord(mine, offset: heapBatchCountOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(count, right: 1))
	if count == remoteBatchBoxes - 1 'full'
		flushBatch(mine)
	end 'full'
end 'freeOther'

// flushBatch puts the boxes a heap has batched on their heap's list, with one compare-exchange that links the batch's end
// to the list's head, which publishes the links stored before it.
function flushBatch(heap Count)
	let first = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapBatchOffset)
	if first == noBox 'empty'
		return
	end 'empty'
	let owner = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapBatchOwnerOffset)
	let last = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapBatchLastOffset)
	var head = __Raw.loadWord(owner, offset: heapRemoteOffset)
	while true 'push'
		__Raw.storeWord(last, offset: 0, value: head)
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(owner, offset: heapRemoteOffset, expected: head, desired: first)
		if seen == head 'pushed'
			break 'push'
		end 'pushed'
		head = seen
	end 'push'
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapBatchOffset, value: noBox)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapBatchCountOffset, value: 0)
end 'flushBatch'

// takeRemote frees into their classes the boxes that other threads freed of heap, the running thread's, and says whether
// there were any.
function takeRemote(heap Count) returns bool
	if __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapRemoteOffset) == noBox 'none'
		return false
	end 'none'
	var box = __Raw.atomicExchange(heap, offset: heapRemoteOffset, value: noBox)
	while box != noBox 'each'
		let next = __Raw.loadWord(box, offset: 0)
		free(box)
		box = next
	end 'each'
	return true
end 'takeRemote'

// heapMade is heap, or the running thread's new heap when it has none yet.
function heapMade(heap Count) returns Count
	if heap == noHeap 'none'
		return newHeapRecord()
	end 'none'
	return heap
end 'heapMade'

// liveBoxes is the number of boxes allocated and not yet freed, summed over the heaps of the threads that run green
// threads, the main one among them (not the monitor's, which frees its own): read once quiesceForCount has parked every
// other such thread for good, its lock a full barrier after each one's last change, so no heap changes meanwhile.
function liveBoxes() returns Count
	let context = __Raw.threadContext()
	let scheduler = __Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextSchedulerOffset)
	if scheduler == noScheduler 'oneThread'
		return heapLive(__Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextHeapOffset))
	end 'oneThread'
	var live = 0 as Count
	var thread = __Raw.loadWord(scheduler, offset: schedAllThreadsOffset)
	while thread != noThread 'threads'
		live = __Raw.wrappingAdd(live, right: heapLive(__Raw.loadWord(thread, offset: contextHeapOffset)))
		thread = __Raw.loadWord(thread, offset: contextAllNextOffset)
	end 'threads'
	return live
end 'liveBoxes'

// heapLive is what a heap holds out: the slots it carved and the large boxes it holds, less those it freed, the slots on
// its stacks and those on its list. A heap's count can be below 0, a wrapping word, when its thread freed large boxes
// that another allocated; the sum is right.
function heapLive(heap Count) returns Count
	if heap == noHeap 'none'
		return 0
	end 'none'
	var live = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapOutOffset)
	var key = 0 as Count
	while key <= lastKey 'classes'
		let stacked = __Raw.wrappingSub(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: key), right: __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapBasesOffset)))
		live = __Raw.wrappingSub(live, right: stacked shr classStepShift)
		key = __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: classStepBytes)
	end 'classes'
	var box = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapRemoteOffset)
	while box != noBox 'remote'
		live = __Raw.wrappingSub(live, right: 1)
		box = __Raw.loadWord(box, offset: 0)
	end 'remote'
	return __Raw.wrappingSub(live, right: __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapBatchCountOffset))
end 'heapLive'

// The counters that HeapStats reads: each heap counts the boxes its thread allocates and frees and the bytes the
// allocations ask for, a plain add to words of its own record that no other thread writes, and a reading sums the
// threads' heaps, as Go's memstats sums its per-processor counts, so an allocation pays no atomic for them.

// countAlloc counts an allocation of a number of bytes in a heap.
function countAlloc(heap Count, bytes Count)
	addHeapWord(heap, offset: heapAllocsOffset, change: 1)
	addHeapWord(heap, offset: heapAllocBytesOffset, change: bytes)
end 'countAlloc'

// addHeapWord adds a number, which wraps to subtract, to a word of a heap's record.
function addHeapWord(heap Count, offset Count, change Count)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: offset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: offset), right: change))
end 'addHeapWord'

// threadHeapCount is a counter of the running thread's heap, 0 before its first allocation.
function threadHeapCount(offset Count) returns Count
	let heap = __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.threadContext(), offset: contextHeapOffset)
	if heap == noHeap 'none'
		return 0
	end 'none'
	return __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: offset)
end 'threadHeapCount'

// processHeapCount is a counter summed over the heaps of every thread, as liveBoxes walks them. Another thread may add to
// its own while the sum reads it, so the sum is of the moment each word is read.
function processHeapCount(offset Count) returns Count
	let context = __Raw.threadContext()
	let scheduler = __Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextSchedulerOffset)
	if scheduler == noScheduler 'oneThread'
		return threadHeapCount(offset)
	end 'oneThread'
	var sum = 0 as Count
	var thread = __Raw.loadWord(scheduler, offset: schedAllThreadsOffset)
	while thread != noThread 'threads'
		let heap = __Raw.loadWord(thread, offset: contextHeapOffset)
		if heap != noHeap 'counted'
			sum = __Raw.wrappingAdd(sum, right: __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: offset))
		end 'counted'
		thread = __Raw.loadWord(thread, offset: contextAllNextOffset)
	end 'threads'
	return sum
end 'processHeapCount'

function heapThreadAllocs() returns Count
	return threadHeapCount(heapAllocsOffset)
end 'heapThreadAllocs'

function heapThreadFrees() returns Count
	return threadHeapCount(heapFreesOffset)
end 'heapThreadFrees'

function heapThreadBytes() returns Count
	return threadHeapCount(heapAllocBytesOffset)
end 'heapThreadBytes'

function heapProcessAllocs() returns Count
	return processHeapCount(heapAllocsOffset)
end 'heapProcessAllocs'

function heapProcessFrees() returns Count
	return processHeapCount(heapFreesOffset)
end 'heapProcessFrees'

function heapProcessLive() returns Count
	return __Raw.wrappingSub(heapProcessAllocs(), right: heapProcessFrees())
end 'heapProcessLive'

function heapProcessBytes() returns Count
	return processHeapCount(heapAllocBytesOffset)
end 'heapProcessBytes'

// heapScavenge gives the running thread's kept large runs back to the page heap, then gives back to the system every
// committed free page that was free at the pass before as well, and answers the bytes given back: a second call after a
// burst gives back what the first left as recent.
function heapScavenge() returns Count
	let heap = heapMade(__Raw.loadWord(__Raw.threadContext(), offset: contextHeapOffset))
	releaseKept(heap)
	return scavengeNow(heapPages(heap))
end 'heapScavenge'

// countOut adds a number, which wraps to subtract, to the boxes a heap gave out that no stack holds.
function countOut(heap Count, change Count)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapOutOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapOutOffset), right: change))
end 'countOut'

// newHeapRecord makes the running thread's heap: a record of the page heap's pages, which are aligned to heapAlignBytes,
// kept for the life of the process, with every stack the empty one, no chunk yet, and the size of each class's boxes; it
// holds the ladder's constants to the keys and the record they lay out.
function newHeapRecord() returns Count
	let pages = processPages(__Raw.threadContext())
	let heap = pageAlloc(pages, count: pagesFor(heapRecordBytes), zeroFrom: 0, zeroTo: heapRecordBytes)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapPagesOffset, value: pages)
	let empty = __Raw.wrappingAdd(heap, right: heapEmptyStackOffset)
	var key = 0 as Count
	var boxBytes = 0 as Count
	var base = linearLimitBytes
	while key <= lastKey 'classes'
		__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: key, value: empty)
		__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapLimitsOffset), value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(empty, right: wordBytes))
		__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapBasesOffset), value: empty)
		var slotBoxBytes = boxBytes
		if slotBoxBytes < smallestBoxBytes 'smallest'
			slotBoxBytes = smallestBoxBytes
		end 'smallest'
		__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapBoxBytesOffset), value: slotBoxBytes)
		var step = classStepBytes
		if boxBytes >= linearLimitBytes 'doubling'
			if boxBytes == __Raw.wrappingAdd(base, right: base) 'power'
				base = boxBytes
			end 'power'
			step = base / classesPerDoubling
		end 'doubling'
		boxBytes = __Raw.wrappingAdd(boxBytes, right: step)
		key = __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: classStepBytes)
	end 'classes'
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(heapLargeKey, right: heapLimitsOffset), value: wordBytes)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapLargeVictimOffset, value: heapLargeSlotsOffset)
	if __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(lastKey, right: heapBoxBytesOffset)) != largestSmallBytes or doublingKey(largestSmallBytes) != lastKey 'ladder'
		panic("heap: the class ladder does not end at lastKey")
	end 'ladder'
	if lastKey >= heapOutOffset or heapLargeKey >= heapLimitsOffset or __Raw.wrappingAdd(heapKeyMask, right: 1) != heapAlignBytes or heapLargeKey >= heapAlignBytes or heapLargeSlotsOffset >= heapRecordBytes or (heap and heapKeyMask) != 0 'layout'
		panic("heap: the classes' keys do not fit the heap's record")
	end 'layout'
	__Raw.storeWord(__Raw.threadContext(), offset: contextHeapOffset, value: heap)
	return heap
end 'newHeapRecord'

// processPages is the process's page heap: the one the main thread's heap names, or a new one when the main thread has no
// heap yet, which is only before its first allocation, while it is the one thread there is (a scheduler is made by
// allocations of the main thread's).
function processPages(context Count) returns Count
	let main = __Raw.loadWord(mainContext(context), offset: contextHeapOffset)
	if main == noHeap 'first'
		return newPageHeap()
	end 'first'
	return heapPages(main)
end 'processPages'

// runningPages is the page heap, by the running thread's heap, made if it has none.
function runningPages() returns Count
	return heapPages(heapMade(__Raw.loadWord(__Raw.threadContext(), offset: contextHeapOffset)))
end 'runningPages'

// doublingKey is the key of the class of a box of more than linearLimitBytes and at most largestSmallBytes: the power of
// two above it, then the step of that power's classes that holds it.
function doublingKey(bytes Count) returns Count
	var base = linearLimitBytes
	var stepShift = firstDoublingStepShift
	var key = linearLimitBytes
	while bytes > __Raw.wrappingAdd(base, right: base) 'power'
		base = __Raw.wrappingAdd(base, right: base)
		stepShift = __Raw.wrappingAdd(stepShift, right: 1)
		key = __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: classesPerDoubling * classStepBytes)
	end 'power'
	let past = __Raw.wrappingSub(__Raw.wrappingSub(bytes, right: base), right: 1)
	let stepKeys = (past shr __Raw.wrappingSub(stepShift, right: classStepShift)) and wordRoundMask
	return __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: __Raw.wrappingAdd(stepKeys, right: classStepBytes))
end 'doublingKey'

// allocFresh is a new box of a class whose stack is empty: one that another thread freed, once the heap has taken its
// list, or else the next slot of the chunk, which starts zeroed. A chunk too short for the slot is left, its tail with it,
// for a new one.
function allocFresh(heap Count, key Count, bytes Count) returns Count
	if takeRemote(heap) and __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: key), offset: 0) != noBox 'freed'
		return alloc(bytes)
	end 'freed'
	let slotBytes = __Raw.wrappingAdd(boxHeaderBytes, right: __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapBoxBytesOffset)))
	var slot = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapNextOffset)
	if __Raw.wrappingSub(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapEndOffset), right: slot) < slotBytes 'chunk'
		slot = newChunk(heap)
	end 'chunk'
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapNextOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(slot, right: slotBytes))
	__Raw.storeWord(slot, offset: 0, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(heap, right: key))
	countOut(heap, change: 1)
	return __Raw.wrappingAdd(slot, right: boxHeaderBytes)
end 'allocFresh'

// newChunk gives heap a new chunk, which its classes carve from on, and gives its start: firstChunkBytes the first time and
// twice the last up to chunkBytes, so a thread that allocates little holds little. Its slots start zeroed.
function newChunk(heap Count) returns Count
	var bytes = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapChunkBytesOffset)
	if bytes == 0 'first'
		bytes = firstChunkBytes
	end 'first'
	let chunk = pageAlloc(heapPages(heap), count: bytes shr pageShift, zeroFrom: 0, zeroTo: bytes)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapEndOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(chunk, right: bytes))
	if bytes < chunkBytes 'grow'
		__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapChunkBytesOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(bytes, right: bytes))
	end 'grow'
	return chunk
end 'newChunk'

// growStack gives a class whose stack is full one twice the size, or firstStackBytes in place of the empty one, holding
// what the old one held, and gives the word above its top.
function growStack(heap Count, key Count) returns Count
	let baseAt = __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapBasesOffset)
	let limitAt = __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapLimitsOffset)
	let base = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: baseAt)
	let used = __Raw.wrappingSub(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: limitAt), right: base)
	let empty = __Raw.wrappingAdd(heap, right: heapEmptyStackOffset)
	var grown = __Raw.wrappingAdd(used, right: used)
	if base == empty 'first'
		grown = firstStackBytes
	end 'first'
	let pages = heapPages(heap)
	let bigger = pageAlloc(pages, count: pagesFor(grown), zeroFrom: 0, zeroTo: 0)
	if used != 0 'used'
		copyBytes(base, target: bigger, count: used)
	end 'used'
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: baseAt, value: bigger)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: limitAt, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(bigger, right: grown))
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: key, value: __Raw.wrappingSub(__Raw.wrappingAdd(bigger, right: used), right: wordBytes))
	if base != empty 'old'
		pageFree(pages, run: base, count: pagesFor(used))
	end 'old'
	return __Raw.wrappingAdd(bigger, right: used)
end 'growStack'

// freeLarge takes a large box out of the count of heap, the freeing thread's, and keeps its run in heap for a large box to
// come.
function freeLarge(heap Count, box Count)
	countOut(heap, change: __Raw.wrappingSub(0, right: 1))
	let block = __Raw.wrappingSub(box, right: largeBoxPadBytes)
	keepLarge(heap, block: block, length: __Raw.loadWord(block, offset: largeBoxLengthOffset))
end 'freeLarge'

// boxCapacity is how many bytes a box holds, which may be more than alloc was asked for: the size of its class's slots, or
// what a large box's run holds past its pad. A String that appends in place grows into it (strings.maxon).
function boxCapacity(box Count) returns Count
	let header = __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.wrappingSub(box, right: boxHeaderBytes), offset: 0)
	let key = header and heapKeyMask
	if key == heapLargeKey 'large'
		let block = __Raw.wrappingSub(box, right: largeBoxPadBytes)
		return __Raw.wrappingSub(__Raw.loadWord(block, offset: largeBoxLengthOffset), right: largeBoxPadBytes)
	end 'large'
	return __Raw.loadWord(header and heapRecordMask, offset: __Raw.wrappingAdd(key, right: heapBoxBytesOffset))
end 'boxCapacity'

// allocLarge is a box of more than largestSmallBytes whose bytes from zeroFrom on are zero: a run that heap kept, or else a
// new run of the page heap, which zeroes only the pages that were written, with the header before the box. The run's first
// word holds its length, which boxCapacity reads and giving it back takes.
function allocLarge(heap Count, bytes Count, zeroFrom Count) returns Count
	countOut(heap, change: 1)
	let length = largeBlockBytes(bytes)
	var block = takeKept(heap, length: length)
	if block != noBox 'kept'
		zeroRange(block, from: __Raw.wrappingAdd(largeBoxPadBytes, right: zeroFrom), to: __Raw.wrappingAdd(largeBoxPadBytes, right: bytes))
	end 'kept' else 'new'
		block = pageAlloc(heapPages(heap), count: length shr pageShift, zeroFrom: __Raw.wrappingAdd(largeBoxPadBytes, right: zeroFrom), zeroTo: __Raw.wrappingAdd(largeBoxPadBytes, right: bytes))
		__Raw.storeWord(block, offset: largeBoxLengthOffset, value: length)
	end 'new'
	let box = __Raw.wrappingAdd(block, right: largeBoxPadBytes)
	__Raw.storeWord(__Raw.wrappingSub(box, right: boxHeaderBytes), offset: 0, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(heap, right: heapLargeKey))
	return box
end 'allocLarge'

// largeBlockBytes is the length of the run of a large box of a number of bytes: the box and its pad in whole pages, which
// the run holds all of, so that a kept run serves any box its pages hold.
function largeBlockBytes(bytes Count) returns Count
	return runBytes(pagesFor(__Raw.wrappingAdd(bytes, right: largeBoxPadBytes)))
end 'largeBlockBytes'

// takeKept takes out of heap's kept runs the shortest that holds length bytes and is at most a quarter longer, so that a
// box never holds much more than it needs, or gives noBox.
function takeKept(heap Count, length Count) returns Count
	let longest = __Raw.wrappingAdd(length, right: length shr largeKeptWasteShift)
	var best = noSlot
	var bestLength = 0 as Count
	var at = heapLargeSlotsOffset
	while at < heapRecordBytes 'slots'
		let block = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: at)
		if block != noBox 'kept'
			let held = __Raw.loadWord(block, offset: largeBoxLengthOffset)
			if held >= length and held <= longest and (best == noSlot or held < bestLength) 'fits'
				best = at
				bestLength = held
			end 'fits'
		end 'kept'
		at = __Raw.wrappingAdd(at, right: wordBytes)
	end 'slots'
	if best == noSlot 'none'
		return noBox
	end 'none'
	let block = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: best)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: best, value: noBox)
	addKept(heap, change: __Raw.wrappingSub(0, right: bestLength))
	return block
end 'takeKept'

// keepLarge keeps a freed run of length bytes in heap for a large box to come. A heap keeps a run in each of its slots and
// at most largeKeptBytes in all: a longer run goes straight back to the page heap, and to make room the heap gives back the
// runs it kept, round its slots, so the one kept longest goes first and a heap that frees much holds no more than the
// bound.
function keepLarge(heap Count, block Count, length Count)
	if length > largeKeptBytes 'long'
		pageFree(heapPages(heap), run: block, count: length shr pageShift)
		return
	end 'long'
	var free = emptySlot(heap)
	while free == noSlot or __Raw.wrappingAdd(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapLargeKeptOffset), right: length) > largeKeptBytes 'room'
		let victim = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapLargeVictimOffset)
		var next = __Raw.wrappingAdd(victim, right: wordBytes)
		if next == heapRecordBytes 'round'
			next = heapLargeSlotsOffset
		end 'round'
		__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapLargeVictimOffset, value: next)
		if __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: victim) != noBox 'giveBack'
			giveBackKept(heap, slot: victim)
			if free == noSlot 'emptied'
				free = victim
			end 'emptied'
		end 'giveBack'
	end 'room'
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: free, value: block)
	addKept(heap, change: length)
end 'keepLarge'

// releaseKept gives every run that a thread's heap keeps back to the page heap: the thread stops for want of work, and an
// idle thread holds no memory for runs to come, which the page heap's release pass can then give back to the system.
function releaseKept(heap Count)
	if heap == noHeap or __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapLargeKeptOffset) == 0 'none'
		return
	end 'none'
	var at = heapLargeSlotsOffset
	while at < heapRecordBytes 'slots'
		if __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: at) != noBox 'kept'
			giveBackKept(heap, slot: at)
		end 'kept'
		at = __Raw.wrappingAdd(at, right: wordBytes)
	end 'slots'
end 'releaseKept'

// giveBackKept gives the run that a slot of heap's kept runs holds back to the page heap.
function giveBackKept(heap Count, slot Count)
	let block = __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: slot)
	let length = __Raw.loadWord(block, offset: largeBoxLengthOffset)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: slot, value: noBox)
	addKept(heap, change: __Raw.wrappingSub(0, right: length))
	pageFree(heapPages(heap), run: block, count: length shr pageShift)
end 'giveBackKept'

// emptySlot is the offset of a slot of heap's kept large runs that holds none, or noSlot.
function emptySlot(heap Count) returns Count
	var at = heapLargeSlotsOffset
	while at < heapRecordBytes 'slots'
		if __Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: at) == noBox 'empty'
			return at
		end 'empty'
		at = __Raw.wrappingAdd(at, right: wordBytes)
	end 'slots'
	return noSlot
end 'emptySlot'

// addKept adds a number, which wraps to subtract, to the bytes of large runs that heap keeps.
function addKept(heap Count, change Count)
	__Raw.storeWord(heap, offset: heapLargeKeptOffset, value: __Raw.wrappingAdd(__Raw.loadWord(heap, offset: heapLargeKeptOffset), right: change))
end 'addKept'

// keep is a zeroed block of the page heap that the runtime keeps until the process ends, such as a thread's context and a
// processor, which no program drops: it is not one of the program's boxes, so the leak check does not count it.
function keep(bytes Count) returns Count
	return pageAlloc(runningPages(), count: pagesFor(bytes), zeroFrom: 0, zeroTo: bytes)
end 'keep'

// unkeep gives back a block of a number of bytes that keep made, which nothing reads again, as a grown table's old one.
function unkeep(block Count, bytes Count)
	pageFree(runningPages(), run: block, count: pagesFor(bytes))
end 'unkeep'
